WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.677
This lesson becomes much easier when the learner stops using one casual motion phrase to cover several different physics quantities.

2
00:00:05.677 --> 00:00:11.353
Displacement tells where the object is relative to the start.

3
00:00:11.353 --> 00:00:17.030
Velocity tells how position changes.

4
00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:22.707
Acceleration tells how velocity changes.

5
00:00:22.707 --> 00:00:28.383
In two dimensions, each of those can also be split into horizontal and vertical components.

6
00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:34.060
That means the safest way to read the motion is to give each quantity its own place on each axis.

7
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:39.737
Once that is done, the overall path becomes much less mysterious.

8
00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:45.413
Keep this takeaway in view: Two-dimensional kinematics becomes safer when each quantity keeps its own component ledger.

9
00:00:45.413 --> 00:00:51.090
Displacement means where now.

10
00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:56.767
Position change should not be confused with the rate of change or the change of rate.

11
00:00:56.767 --> 00:01:02.443
Velocity means how position changes.

12
00:01:02.443 --> 00:01:08.120
Velocity belongs to the motion story itself and can differ by axis.

13
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:15.509
This diagram helps because it prevents the learner from collapsing several ideas into one arrow.

14
00:01:15.509 --> 00:01:22.898
A path can curve even when one component behaves simply.

15
00:01:22.898 --> 00:01:30.286
Velocity can point one way while acceleration points another.

16
00:01:30.286 --> 00:01:37.675
Displacement can be read from start to finish even if the object did not travel in a straight line.

17
00:01:37.675 --> 00:01:45.064
The diagram therefore trains the eye to respect the separate jobs of these quantities before trying to tell one combined motion story.

18
00:01:45.064 --> 00:01:52.452
Keep this takeaway in view: A good kinematics map gives every quantity its own role before recombining the full motion.

19
00:01:52.452 --> 00:01:59.841
Displacement: Start-to-finish change keeps a different meaning from the rate-based quantities.

20
00:01:59.841 --> 00:02:07.230
Velocity: Component velocity tells how motion proceeds on each axis at that instant.

21
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:12.119
A helpful analogy is a logistics dashboard.

22
00:02:12.119 --> 00:02:17.008
One panel shows where the vehicle is.

23
00:02:17.008 --> 00:02:21.898
Another shows how fast and in what direction it is moving.

24
00:02:21.898 --> 00:02:26.787
Another shows how that motion is changing.

25
00:02:26.787 --> 00:02:31.676
If those panels were merged into one vague description, the report would become much less useful.

26
00:02:31.676 --> 00:02:36.565
Two-dimensional kinematics works the same way.

27
00:02:36.565 --> 00:02:41.454
Displacement, velocity, and acceleration should be kept in separate slots so the learner can trust what each statement actually means.

28
00:02:41.454 --> 00:02:46.343
Keep this takeaway in view: The dashboard analogy helps because it makes separate motion quantities feel necessary rather than optional.

29
00:02:46.343 --> 00:02:51.233
Location panel means displacement.

30
00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:56.122
This answers where the object is relative to a reference point.

31
00:02:56.122 --> 00:03:01.011
Motion panel means velocity.

32
00:03:01.011 --> 00:03:05.900
This answers how position is changing on the axes.

33
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:12.753
The central move is not a new equation but a new way of organizing them.

34
00:03:12.753 --> 00:03:19.607
The standard kinematics relations can be applied on each axis.

35
00:03:19.607 --> 00:03:26.460
Horizontal displacement can be linked to horizontal velocity and horizontal acceleration.

36
00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:33.313
Vertical motion can be treated separately with its own values.

37
00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:40.167
The lesson therefore does not ask the learner to memorize a new universe of formulas.

38
00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:47.020
It asks the learner to use familiar relations in two separate ledgers and recombine the story only after that.

39
00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:53.873
Keep this takeaway in view: Two-dimensional formulas are strongest when they are treated as separate axis ledgers before recombination.

40
00:03:53.873 --> 00:04:00.727
Zero on one axis: A zero acceleration or velocity component in one direction does not erase what is happening on the other.

41
00:04:00.727 --> 00:04:07.580
Reuse familiar tools: Two-dimensional motion often becomes simpler when the learner sees it as paired one-dimensional problems.

42
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:14.433
apply kinematics relations on each axis separately.

43
00:04:14.433 --> 00:04:21.287
let horizontal and vertical quantities keep their own values.

44
00:04:21.287 --> 00:04:28.140
The safest two-dimensional method is often familiar one-dimensional physics used twice, once on each axis.

45
00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:34.063
A strong routine is to label the quantity being asked about first.

46
00:04:34.063 --> 00:04:39.986
Then split the motion into horizontal and vertical components.

47
00:04:39.986 --> 00:04:45.909
Then solve the component stories with the appropriate relations.

48
00:04:45.909 --> 00:04:51.832
Only after that should the learner recombine or describe the full motion.

49
00:04:51.832 --> 00:04:57.755
This order matters because many mistakes come from solving before the quantity has even been named properly.

50
00:04:57.755 --> 00:05:03.678
Keep this takeaway in view: Label-split-solve is the safest two-dimensional routine because it protects the meaning of each quantity.

51
00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:09.601
Step 1 means label the quantity.

52
00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:15.524
Decide whether the question is about displacement, velocity, or acceleration before anything else.

53
00:05:15.524 --> 00:05:21.447
Step 2 means split the axes.

54
00:05:21.447 --> 00:05:27.370
Write separate horizontal and vertical stories instead of one blended motion statement.

55
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:32.785
This extra scene matters because the learner often knows the words displacement, velocity, and acceleration but still swaps them in practice.

56
00:05:32.785 --> 00:05:38.200
A state table view fixes that.

57
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.615
Position tells where the object is.

58
00:05:43.615 --> 00:05:49.030
Velocity tells how fast and in what direction position is changing.

59
00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:54.445
Acceleration tells how that velocity is changing.

60
00:05:54.445 --> 00:05:59.860
When those three are written in separate rows or columns, the motion problem becomes much more stable.

61
00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:05.275
The learner can then ask which row belongs to the question instead of reaching for the wrong idea under pressure.

62
00:06:05.275 --> 00:06:10.690
Keep this takeaway in view: The state-table view helps because it turns three slippery words into three distinct motion roles.

63
00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:16.105
Position row means where.

64
00:06:16.105 --> 00:06:21.520
This row describes the object's location or displacement state.

65
00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.935
Velocity row means how it moves.

66
00:06:26.935 --> 00:06:32.350
This row describes how position changes over time on each axis.

67
00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:36.103
Try four checks.

68
00:06:36.103 --> 00:06:39.857
Are displacement and velocity the same quantity?

69
00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:43.610
No.

70
00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:47.363
Can acceleration exist on one axis while being zero on another?

71
00:06:47.363 --> 00:06:51.117
Yes.

72
00:06:51.117 --> 00:06:54.870
Should two-dimensional motion be split into component stories before recombining?

73
00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:58.623
Yes.

74
00:06:58.623 --> 00:07:02.377
And should the learner decide which quantity is being discussed before choosing an equation?

75
00:07:02.377 --> 00:07:06.130
Yes.

76
00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:09.883
Those checks show whether the motion ledger is becoming reliable.

77
00:07:09.883 --> 00:07:13.637
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can defend two-dimensional motion as separate component ledgers.

78
00:07:13.637 --> 00:07:17.390
Quantity check means not the same.

79
00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:21.143
Displacement, velocity, and acceleration should not be blended into one label.

80
00:07:21.143 --> 00:07:24.897
Axis check means different stories.

81
00:07:24.897 --> 00:07:28.650
One axis can behave simply while the other carries the changing part of the motion.

82
00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:35.275
You are ready for the worked example now.

83
00:07:35.275 --> 00:07:41.899
Start by naming which quantity the question is asking for.

84
00:07:41.899 --> 00:07:48.524
Then split the motion into horizontal and vertical component stories.

85
00:07:48.524 --> 00:07:55.148
Then solve those stories with the appropriate relations and only afterward rebuild the whole answer if needed.

86
00:07:55.148 --> 00:08:01.773
The best answers in this lesson sound like disciplined motion bookkeeping rather than like improvised equation grabbing.

87
00:08:01.773 --> 00:08:08.397
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example becomes much safer when two-dimensional motion is solved as separate quantity and component ledgers.

88
00:08:08.397 --> 00:08:15.022
Do not blur the quantities: Most errors in this lesson begin when displacement, velocity, and acceleration are used interchangeably.

89
00:08:15.022 --> 00:08:21.646
Trust the components: If the path looks complicated, the component ledgers are often simpler than the full picture.

90
00:08:21.646 --> 00:08:28.271
name the physical quantity first.

91
00:08:28.271 --> 00:08:34.895
separate the horizontal and vertical stories.

92
00:08:34.895 --> 00:08:41.520
A strong solution keeps quantity meaning and axis meaning visible from first line to last.
